    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1262">

                <pb facs="1367" n="1357" sameAs="frus:frus1944v04/pg1357"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 Stettinius Mission/29: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-04-15T18:00:00+02:00">April 15, 1944—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received April 15—3:15 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3088. Deles<anchor n="64" xml:id="d1262-f1"/> No. 13. In touching briefly on the
                    Yugoslav problem and in reply to our question Sir Orme Sargent<anchor n="65"
                        xml:id="d1262-f2"/> remarked that somewhat to British surprise King Peter’s
                        marriage<anchor n="66" xml:id="d1262-f3"/> had been subject to considerable
                    disapproval in Yugoslavia and particularly in old Serbia. He feels that it is
                    too early to estimate how seriously this has affected the King’s standing among
                    the bulk of the Serbian population but that the marriage has certainly not
                    increased his prestige or popularity.</p>
                <pb facs="1368" n="1358" xml:id="pg1358"/>
                <p>Both Mr. Eden and Sargent have admitted in conversation that there is no doubt of
                    Mihailovich’s great popularity in Serbia. Their information confirms the
                    Department’s feeling that he is regarded almost as a Messiah in that area.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Winant</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1262-f1" n="64" xml:id="d1262-f1-content">
                <p>Designation for a series of telegrams from the Under Secretary of State to the
                    Department in connection with his mission to London in April 1944. For the Under
                    Secretary’s report to the Secretary of State on his conversations in London,
                    April 7 to April 29, 1944, see <ref target="frus1944v03#pg_1">vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">iii</hi>, pp. 1</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1262-f2" n="65" xml:id="d1262-f2-content">
                <p>Sir Orme G. Sargent, Deputy Under Secretary of State in the British Foreign
                    Office.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1262-f3" n="66" xml:id="d1262-f3-content">
                <p>King Peter was married to Princess Alexandra of Greece in London on March 20,
                    1944.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
